    history: 'Coyoacán is one of the oldest continuously inhabited settlements in the Valley of Mexico, dating back to pre-Hispanic times when it was a major Tepanec and later Mexica (Aztec) town. Its name comes from the Nahuatl coyohuacan, meaning "place of coyotes." After the fall of Tenochtitlán in 1521, Hernán Cortés briefly made Coyoacán the first seat of the Spanish colonial government while the ruined Aztec capital was being rebuilt, making it effectively the first capital of New Spain. For the next four centuries, it remained a distinct pueblo with its own town hall, cobblestone streets, and colonial plazas, and was formally incorporated into Mexico City only in 1928. In the 20th century, Coyoacán became famous as the home of Frida Kahlo (whose Casa Azul is now one of the city\'s most visited museums), Diego Rivera, and the exiled Russian revolutionary Leon Trotsky, who was assassinated here in 1940. Today it retains a remarkably preserved small-town colonial charm.',
