    history: 'The Distillery District occupies the grounds of the former Gooderham & Worts distillery, founded in 1832 by James Worts and William Gooderham. What started as a small wind-powered mill grew into the largest distillery in the British Empire and the largest producer of whisky in the world by the late 19th century. The site contains over 40 Victorian-era industrial buildings — the largest and best-preserved collection of Victorian industrial architecture in North America. The distillery operated until 1990, when production was moved elsewhere. The site was acquired by developers who set about transforming it into a pedestrian-only cultural and commercial district. After extensive restoration, the Distillery District opened to the public in 2003. Today it is a National Historic Site of Canada and one of Toronto\'s top tourist destinations — home to art galleries, boutique shops, restaurants, cafes, theaters, and event venues. The annual Toronto Christmas Market in the Distillery District has become one of the city\'s most beloved holiday traditions, drawing hundreds of thousands of visitors each year.',
