    gyms: 'Limited gym options compared to other areas. Some local gyms and hotel fitness centers. Most expats who live in the Old City commute to gyms elsewhere or use outdoor exercise (walking, running in Sanam Luang and Lumphini Park). Yoga studios are available but fewer than in trendy areas.',
    healthcare: 'Siriraj Hospital (major public/teaching hospital) is across the river on the Thonburi side. There are local clinics and pharmacies, but the area has fewer premium private healthcare options than Sukhumvit. For serious or complex care, most expats go to Bumrungrad or other private hospitals in the Sukhumvit area — about a 30–60 minute drive depending on traffic.',
    expatCommunity: 'The Old City has a small expat community — mostly people who are interested in Thai culture, history, art, or Buddhism. There are expat artists, writers, retirees, and people working in NGOs or cultural organizations. It is not a typical "expat bubble" neighborhood — you will need more Thai language skills and be more integrated with local life. The community is smaller and more tight-knit than Sukhumvit. Khao San Road draws a lot of backpacker tourists but most are short-term. Americans in the area tend to be longer-term residents who have chosen the Old City specifically for its culture and history.',
