    expatCommunity: 'Ladadika has a small expat presence — mostly younger expats, students, and people who work in hospitality. It is not primarily a residential neighborhood, so fewer expats live here full-time. Most expats in Thessaloniki live in Kalamaria, Toumba, or other residential neighborhoods and come to Ladadika for nights out. The expats who do live here tend to be short-term, young, and drawn by the nightlife and creative scene. You will hear English in the bars and restaurants but mostly from tourists and students.',
    history: "Ladadika gets its name from the Greek word 'ladi' (oil) — the area was originally the olive oil and food market district of Thessaloniki during the Ottoman period (15th–19th centuries). Its narrow streets, stone warehouses, and arched passages were built for merchants and traders who imported and stored goods arriving at the nearby port. The neighborhood was one of the oldest commercial districts of the city, with a multicultural population of Greeks, Jews, Turks, and others. After the Great Fire of 1917 destroyed much of central Thessaloniki, Ladadika survived relatively intact, preserving its historic street plan and architecture. Through much of the 20th century, the area declined — warehouses fell into disrepair and the neighborhood had a rough reputation. In the 1980s and 1990s, Ladadika began to be rediscovered — old warehouses were converted into bars, restaurants, and clubs, and the area became the heart of Thessaloniki's nightlife. Today, it is one of the most popular areas in the city for dining, drinking, and live music — a place where Ottoman-era warehouses host modern bars and restaurants, and history meets nightlife.",
