    propertyPlatforms: ['Spitogatos', 'XE.gr', 'ReMax Greece', 'Facebook marketplace'],
    gyms: 'Good fitness options in Kalamaria. Several gyms, yoga studios, and Pilates studios. The waterfront promenade is excellent for walking, jogging, and cycling — many people exercise outdoors. Beach areas have outdoor fitness equipment in some places. The area has tennis clubs and other sports facilities. For a suburban area, there are plenty of fitness options. Outdoor swimming pools are available in summer.',
    healthcare: 'Good healthcare in Kalamaria. Several private clinics and diagnostic centers. Many doctors and dentists have offices in the area. Pharmacies are everywhere. Major hospitals in Thessaloniki are accessible by bus or car — about 15–30 minutes depending on traffic. For routine care, you can find everything locally. For specialized care, you go into the city center. The area has all standard healthcare services.',
    expatCommunity: 'Kalamaria has a growing expat community — one of the most popular areas for expats in Thessaloniki. It attracts professionals, families, and people who prefer a quieter, safer, more suburban lifestyle. The community includes Europeans, Americans, and other nationalities. Many expats working for international companies or consulates live here. The expat community is more family-oriented and professional than in neighborhoods like Ladadika or Toumba. It is less visible and less of a party scene — people connect through schools, sports, and neighborhood events. English is heard on the streets but Greek is still the main language.',
    history: "Kalamaria was originally a small fishing village and summer resort area outside Thessaloniki. In antiquity, the area had several small settlements. During the Ottoman period, it remained mostly agricultural with a few fishing villages. The area had a mixed population — Greeks, Turks, and Jews. After the population exchange between Greece and Turkey in 1923, many Greek refugees from Asia Minor settled in Kalamaria, which led to significant population growth. In the second half of the 20th century, Kalamaria grew rapidly as Thessaloniki expanded — apartment buildings replaced fields and orchards. The area developed into a major residential suburb. In recent decades, it has become increasingly upscale, especially along the waterfront. New residential developments, shops, and restaurants have transformed the area. Today, Kalamaria is the second most populous municipality in the Thessaloniki urban area — a modern, family-oriented suburb with a high quality of life and a long waterfront promenade along the Thermaic Gulf.",
    jobsForExpats: [
      { title: 'Professional / Corporate', salary: '€1,000 – 3,000+', sectors: 'Business', description: 'Some international companies have offices in Thessaloniki. Kalamaria is a popular place to live for professionals who work in the city. Shipping, manufacturing, and services are major industries. English-speaking professionals with specialized skills can sometimes find positions. Salaries are generally lower than in Athens or Western Europe but go further due to lower cost of living.' },
      { title: 'Remote Work / Digital Nomad', salary: 'Varies — US pay', sectors: 'Remote', description: 'A growing number of remote workers choose Kalamaria for its quality of life, safety, and affordability. Good internet, quiet residential environment, and easy access to the sea. The main trade-off is that you are not in the center of the action — fewer cafes, co-working spaces, and social events. Many remote workers who value peace and quiet prefer Kalamaria to the city center. Commuting to the center takes 15–30 minutes.' },
      { title: 'Education / Teaching', salary: '€700 – 1,400/month', sectors: 'Education', description: 'International schools and language schools in Thessaloniki hire English teachers. Private tutoring is popular. Families with children often choose Kalamaria for its schools and safe environment. Teachers who work at international schools can earn decent salaries — especially compared to local wages. University positions are also available at Aristotle University and other institutions.' },
      { title: 'Healthcare / Medical', salary: '€1,000 – 2,500+', sectors: 'Healthcare', description: 'Hospitals and clinics in Thessaloniki hire medical professionals. Private clinics sometimes seek English-speaking doctors and nurses for their international patient departments. Medical licensing in Greece can be a complex process for foreign-trained professionals, but it is possible in some fields. Pharmacists and other allied health professionals also have opportunities.' },
      { title: 'Retirement / FIP', salary: 'N/A (pension income)', sectors: 'Retirement', description: 'Kalamaria is a popular choice for retirees — safe, clean, with good healthcare access and a calm atmosphere. The seaside location is a big plus. Combined with the Non-Dom tax regime and low cost of living, retirees can live very comfortably. The area has everything you need for daily life within walking distance or a short bus ride.' },
    ],
    jobWarning: 'Kalamaria is primarily residential — most people who live here work in the city center or other parts of Thessaloniki. There are not many major employers located in Kalamaria itself. Job opportunities are generally found elsewhere in the metropolitan area. The local economy is oriented toward serving residents — shops, restaurants, services — rather than major industries. For expats, remote work, teaching, and retirement are the most common paths. Greek language is important for most local jobs.',
