    history: 'EUR (Esposizione Universale Roma) was conceived in the 1930s by Benito Mussolini\'s Fascist regime as the site for a planned World\'s Fair (Esposizione Universale) in 1942, intended to celebrate 20 years of Fascism and showcase the "new Rome." The district was designed in the Rationalist architectural style — monumental, geometric, and stripped-down — with wide boulevards, imposing stone buildings, and strict symmetry. Construction began in the mid-1930s but was interrupted by World War II, and the World\'s Fair never took place. After the war, the district was repurposed and gradually developed as a business and residential district. Many government ministries, corporate headquarters, and international organizations moved into the grand Fascist-era buildings. Today EUR is one of Rome\'s most modern business districts, with glass office towers alongside the historic rationalist architecture. It is also a popular residential area for middle-class and affluent families who appreciate its green spaces, order, and relative quiet compared to chaotic central Rome. The iconic Palazzo della Civiltà Italiana (also known as the "Square Colosseum") is the district\'s most famous building — currently the headquarters of Fendi.',
